      "question": "What specific conditions, severity thresholds, or detection sources cause an event to be formally classified as a defect that initiates this triage workflow?",
      "answer": "The workflow begins when someone with ownership of the problem submits an initial report backed by early evidence, including a description of the fault, context about the affected batch or sample, and an assumption about customer impact. The specific numeric thresholds or detection sources that trigger formal classification are not specified in the available information."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q2",
      "question": "Which roles or teams are required to participate in the discussion step, and who holds the authority to approve moving the defect to the experimentation phase?",
      "answer": "After the fault is logged, a quality-focused engineer assigns a severity level and determines scope. The person who owns the issue then alerts relevant teams and assigns responsibilities. A technical engineer focused on the process subsequently defines root-cause hypotheses and moves into experimentation. Explicit approval authority for advancing to the experiment phase is not specified."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q3",
      "question": "What types of experiments are performed, and what evidence or outcome criteria must be met before the experimentation step is considered complete?",
      "answer": "The process engineer devises and executes follow-up experiments aimed at testing root-cause hypotheses. Completion is evaluated by whether the experimental data supports or contradicts the proposed hypothesis—those are the two defined outcome branches. Specific experiment types or quantitative completion criteria are not detailed in the available information."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q4",
      "question": "What decision criteria or evaluation framework is used to select an improvement action from the experimental results, and are there predefined categories of improvement options?",
      "answer": "The issue owner reviews the experimental findings and chooses a corrective action based on whether the results validated the hypothesis. If the hypothesis is not supported, the investigation loop presumably continues. Predefined categories of improvement options or a formal scoring framework are not specified in the available information."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q5",
      "question": "Who are the intended recipients of the risk communication, and is there a required format or mandatory content structure for that report?",
      "answer": "A person responsible for the customer relationship evaluates exposure to the customer, and a manager then distributes the concluding report along with assigned action items. A standardized template is referenced as a tool in the process. Mandatory content fields within that template are not specified in the available information."
    },
    {
      "question_id": "Q6",
      "question": "Are there defined time limits or service-level targets for each stage of the triage process, and what formally marks a defect case as resolved or closed?",
      "answer": "Time limits or service-level targets for individual stages are not specified. The final activity in the workflow is the manager distributing the concluding report and action items, which appears to represent the end of the process, but a formal closure criterion or sign-off mechanism is not explicitly defined."
    }
  ]
}
